<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/baseline-profiles.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">A newly installed app feels slower on its first useful journey than after repeated use. If runtime compilation contributes to that difference, a profile can tell ART which paths deserve preparation. It still needs to match the shipped code.</p>

Follow the profile from an exercised journey to the release artifact, the device’s compilation state and a measured result.

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->

### Two profiles act at different boundaries

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Baseline Profile</strong><small>Guides ART optimisation of matching classes and methods on the device.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Startup Profile</strong><small>Guides build-time DEX layout for startup paths.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Benchmark evidence</strong><small>Checks the actual result for a chosen journey and compilation state.</small></div></div><figcaption>Neither profile is a new algorithm or an I/O cache. Their value depends on the work the journey actually performs.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Architecture :: animation -->

### A generated profile is only the first link

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A generated profile is only the first link</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="journey" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Journey</strong><small>Exercise useful app paths</small></div><div class="node" data-el="artifact" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Artifact</strong><small>Rules match final code</small></div><div class="node" data-el="device" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Device</strong><small>Verify install / compilation</small></div><div class="node" data-el="measure" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Measurement</strong><small>Compare identical scenarios</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Exercise", "caption": "Generate rules from representative startup and interaction paths. The animation is a dependency checklist, not an install-time trace.", "state": {"journey": "active"}, "add": ["journey", "artifact", "device", "measure"]}, {"label": "Package", "caption": "Build tooling merges and rewrites matching rules for the release artifact. Keep the generated rules and build identity associated.", "state": {"journey": "done", "artifact": "active"}}, {"label": "Verify", "caption": "Confirm the intended profile and compilation state on the test device rather than inferring success from a file’s existence.", "state": {"artifact": "done", "device": "active"}}, {"label": "Compare", "caption": "Measure with and without the intended profile configuration. If another dependency dominates, the journey may show little improvement.", "state": {"device": "done", "measure": "active"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->

Choose a profile journey because a valuable interaction pays avoidable runtime work, not because it is easy to automate. Keep generation inputs and the packaged profile tied to the release artifact. Then compare the same install/compilation conditions and device class with and without the profile. A generated rules file is an intermediate output; the release decision needs evidence that the shipped journey benefits under the conditions users actually encounter.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>A profile file is packaged. Is the speedup established?</summary>

No. Verify installation and compilation, then measure the relevant journey.

</details>

<details><summary>Which profile changes DEX layout during the build?</summary>

The Startup Profile. Baseline Profiles guide ART optimisation on-device.

</details>

<details><summary>What if the trace shows disk waiting dominates?</summary>

Investigate that boundary. A profile does not remove the disk dependency.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Profiles overview](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/baselineprofiles/overview), [Validate profiles](https://developer.android.com/topic/performance/baselineprofiles/create-baselineprofile).
